<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Germanicus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">15 BCE–19 CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a> · Also known as Germanicus Julius Caesar</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Germanicus <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a> (15 BCE–19 CE) was a Roman general, nephew and adopted son of the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiberius">Tiberius</a>. From 14 to 16 CE he led campaigns across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arminius">Arminius</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germanic-peoples">Germanic peoples</a>, visiting the site of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-teutoburg-forest">Teutoburg</a> disaster and recovering two of Varus' lost eagles, but made no lasting conquest. He died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> after a quarrel with its governor Piso, and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caligula">Caligula</a> later became emperor.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/germanicus.jpg" alt="Germanicus" width="320" height="399" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: J. Paul Getty Museum · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bust_of_Germanicus,_front_-_Getty_Museum_(2021.66).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
